new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c1c8a03
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/opencode/src/tool/wait.txt
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+Block until a condition becomes true, then report the result. Use this when you
+genuinely cannot make progress until something external happens 窶・a service on
+another machine finishes booting, a port opens, a deployment goes green, a
+remote job you already started comes back.
+
+This tool never starts anything. It cannot launch a server, a watcher or a
+build, so it cannot leave a process running with nobody watching it. To start
+something, use `bash`. If that command then goes silent, `bash` already moves it
+to the background and hands you its PID 窶・pass that PID here as
+`{ "kind": "process", "pid": N }` to wait for it to finish.
+
+Conditions:
+
+- `{ "kind": "port", "port": 5432, "host": "db.internal" }` 窶・a TCP port accepts
+  a connection. Defaults to 127.0.0.1.
+- `{ "kind": "http", "url": "https://x/health", "status": 200 }` 窶・an HTTP
+  request returns that status. Defaults to 200.
+- `{ "kind": "file", "path": "/tmp/ready", "contains": "listening" }` 窶・a file
+  exists, optionally containing that text.
+- `{ "kind": "process", "pid": 12345 }` 窶・that process has exited. Use this for
+  a job `bash` already backgrounded.
+
+`timeoutMs` bounds the whole wait and defaults to 1 hour. On timeout nothing is
+killed 窶・there is nothing of yours running 窶・and you are told the condition never
+became true, so you can raise the limit or give up deliberately. Set `intervalMs`
+(default 5s) to trade responsiveness against request volume.
+
+Do not use this to wait for your own backgrounded command's output by polling a
+log when the PID is available: `kind: "process"` is exact and cheaper.
